    <h2>A World Building Project</h2> 

    <ul>
  <li>What would a pre-modern fictive world look like culturally and aesthetically on a landmass that is a bit like the Silk Road + northern America + Patagonia?</li>
  <li>What unique and internally consistent aesthetics could we have in fantasy worlds?</li>
  <li>How is a society's environment reflected in their culture?</li>
  <li>What happens at cultural crossroads?</li>
  <li>How to express the sense of awe coming from experiencing historical memory?</li>
 <li>How did the <a href="https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.12987/9780300231687-005/html">domus complex</a> come to take over the world?</li>
</ul>
<br>
<p>These are some of the questions driving my world building project of Kuha, an ancient high fantasy world. I'm interested in building some geographical and environmental earth-like constraints to see what kind of societies might plausibly emerge, and what kind of drama may arise from these.</p>

<h2>About the builder</h2>

<div class="content-block">
  <img src="/images/20260208_Variation.png" alt="Imaginary Yonder Emblem" />
  <div class="content-text">
    <p>My name is Eri. I'm a (former?) academic sociolinguist, artist-in-making, and world builder. I'm experimenting my way through a creative practice that combines world building, writing, and art in the pursuit of exploring our own complex world.</p>
    <p>Find me elsewhere online:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://bsky.app/profile/imaginaryyonder.bsky.social">Bluesky</a> - Unpolished streams of thought, logs of illustration work in progress.</li>
      <li><a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7534-7047">Academic Publications</a> - List of academic linguistic publications in the areas of sociolinguistics, and comparative linguistics.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
